  "title": "Introduction to Exponential Functions",
  "body": " Introduction to Exponential Functions   An exponential function models repeated multiplication. Unlike a linear function, which changes by adding the same amount repeatedly, an exponential function changes by multiplying by the same factor repeatedly.    Exponential Function  An exponential function has the form , where , , and .   is the initial value, or y-intercept.  is the growth or decay factor.     Growth and Decay   Exponential Growth  If , the function represents exponential growth.    Exponential Decay  If , the function represents exponential decay.    Lily Pad Growth   A lake starts with 3 lily pads and the number of lily pads doubles each day.    The initial value is 3 and the growth factor is 2.      Lily Pad Growth Data   Day 0 1 2 3 4  L(t) 3 6 12 24 48      Writing an Exponential Equation  The key pieces of information are the initial value and the growth or decay factor.   Writing an Equation from Context   A population starts at 500 and increases by 8% each year. Write an exponential model.    The initial value is 500.  An increase of 8% corresponds to a growth factor of .      Writing a Decay Equation   A pond contains 400 flowers. Each day, only one-quarter of the flowers remain.    The initial value is 400 and the decay factor is .       Graphs and Properties     The domain of an exponential function is all real numbers.  If , the range is .  The graph has a horizontal asymptote at .  The y-intercept is .     Reading Information from an Equation   Consider .     Initial value: 5  Growth factor: 3  y-intercept: (0,5)  The function represents exponential growth.       Finding an Equation from Points   Known y-Intercept   An exponential function passes through the points and . Find an equation for the function.    Because the graph passes through , the initial value is .  Substitute into .     Therefore,      "
