  "id": "sec-applications-exponential-functions",
  "level": "1",
  "url": "sec-applications-exponential-functions.html",
  "type": "Section",
  "number": "4.2",
  "title": "Applications of Exponential Functions",
  "body": " Applications of Exponential Functions   Exponential functions appear in many applications involving repeated growth or decay. In this section, we interpret exponential models, identify growth and decay rates, and compare exponential functions written with a general base to those written with the natural base .    General Exponential Model  A general exponential model has the form , where is the initial value and is the growth or decay factor.    Natural Exponential Model  A natural exponential model has the form , where is the initial value and represents a continuous growth or decay rate.    Growth and Decay Rates    For , growth occurs when and decay occurs when .  For , growth occurs when and decay occurs when .     General-Base Growth  Determine whether represents growth or decay. Identify the growth rate.   The growth factor is . Since , the model represents growth.  The growth rate is .     Natural-Base Decay  Determine whether represents growth or decay.   The exponent coefficient is . Because it is negative, the model represents continuous exponential decay.  The decay rate is .      Interpreting Exponential Models   Bacteria Population   A bacteria culture is modeled by   Interpret the initial value and growth factor.    The initial population is 500 bacteria.  The factor 1.35 indicates a 35% increase during each time period.     Cooling Coffee   The temperature of a cup of coffee is modeled by   Explain why this model represents decay.    The exponent coefficient is negative, so the exponential portion decreases over time.  The model describes a temperature approaching room temperature.      General Base versus Natural Base   Both forms model exponential change. The difference is how the rate is represented.   Comparing Exponential Forms   General Base Natural Base   Uses growth or decay factor Uses continuous rate  gives growth gives growth  gives decay gives decay     Classifying Exponential Models   For each model, identify whether it is a general-base model or a natural-base model and determine whether it represents growth or decay.           General-base growth.  General-base decay.  Natural-base decay.  Natural-base growth.       Applied Exponential Problems   Medical Isotope Decay   The amount of a medical isotope is modeled by   where is measured in hours.   How much remains after 2 hours?  What is the initial amount?      . About 317 units remain.  The initial amount is .      Population Growth   A wildlife population is modeled by   Interpret the value of 0.08.    The model uses the natural base .  The value 0.08 represents a continuous growth rate of 8% per time period.      Key Ideas    uses a growth or decay factor.  uses a continuous growth or decay rate.  The parameter is the initial value in either form.  Growth occurs when or .  Decay occurs when or .     "
