  "body": " Long-Run Behavior of Rational Functions  In this section, we will concern ourselves with the long-run behavior of rational functions.  Consider the graph of the function       f(x)=(2*x*(x+1))\/((x+2)*(x-1))            Just like with polynomial functions, we can imagine zooming way out on the graph, and we want a way to describe what the far left and far right ends of the graph are doing.      f(x)=(2*x*(x+1))\/((x+2)*(x-1))            This shows us that, when our -values become very large and negative, our -values become very close to , and when our -values become very large and positive, our -values become very close to as well.  Using our symbolic end behavior notation, we can write this as and   But why does this happen? Let's revisit our function's equation: If we multiply out the numerator and the denominator of the function, we can rewrite the function as   "
